# case: 080-selfheal-admits-wildcard
# what: SELF_HEALING_ENABLED=0 turns off the path this case exists to prove
#       alive — no (auto-allowed) line, no ipset add, retry still blocked
#
# The timeout bump is part of the mutation, not incidental. it_wait 60 is
# SIXTY ITERATIONS, not sixty seconds, and each one runs a curl that blocks
# for IT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT (5s) plus a 1s sleep. When self-healing works the
# wait returns on the first or second poll, so the 300s default is ample and
# nobody ever measured the exhausted path. With self-healing OFF all three
# waits run to exhaustion: ~360-420s for the first alone, ~210s for the other
# two. Without this header the case times out at 300s, and a timeout is NOT a
# demonstration — it shows the case is slow, not that the assertion can fail.
#
diff --git a/tests/integration/cases/080-selfheal-admits-wildcard.sh b/tests/integration/cases/080-selfheal-admits-wildcard.sh
index f714af7..6c2fe10 100755
--- a/tests/integration/cases/080-selfheal-admits-wildcard.sh
+++ b/tests/integration/cases/080-selfheal-admits-wildcard.sh
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 #           auto-allowed on the spot, without waiting for the 60s refresh
 # tags:     security network-mode restricted needs-dns
 # requires: docker netadmin sidecar dns
+# timeout:  900
 #
 # THIS IS THE PROPERTY THAT SILENTLY DIED WITH THE CAPTURE DAEMON. Enforcement
 # kept working throughout the original outage, so nothing looked wrong — but
@@ -37,7 +38,7 @@ adir="$(it_scratch)"
 # is the wildcard in the proxy-domains file, matched by the self-healing path —
 # so a successful retry below cannot be explained any other way.
 allowlist_write "$adir" "" "" "*.wild.test"
-sandbox_up restricted "$adir" --dns "$IT_DNS_IP" || it_finish
+sandbox_up restricted "$adir" --dns "$IT_DNS_IP" -e SELF_HEALING_ENABLED=0 || it_finish   # KNOWN-BAD
 
 # Self-healing lives in the NFLOG watcher, and tshark takes ~22s to attach.
 # Firing traffic before then means the drop is never seen, nothing is looked up,
